Puddle-Jump Swing

Creative Catalyst: The sound was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule, which allegedly contained a recording of a duck-calling competition playing over a meditative 432Hz yoga tape.

Puddle-Jump Swing is a disorienting, high-energy yet strangely restorative soundscape. It pairs the jangling, optimistic chord progressions of indie pop with the precise, grounding frequencies of 432Hz meditation music, all underpinned by a glitchy, syncopated rhythm section characterized by "Barking Duck" samples—a percussive, nasal, and rhythmic quacking that mimics the stop-start swing of 1930s jazz. It sounds like a frantic garden party held inside a sensory deprivation tank.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 115–128 BPM; a "limping" swing shuffle that feels like it’s constantly tripping over its own feet before recovering.
*   Harmonic Palette: Major 7th and 9th chords tuned to 432Hz, creating a slightly flat, "watery" resonance that avoids the sharpness of standard tuning.
*   Production Signatures: "Hydro-gating" (sidechaining duck-call samples to the beat), aggressive tape-saturation on brass samples, and extreme panning of melodic indie-pop guitars.
*   Dynamics: A cyclical structure where the meditative, long-form pads swell, only to be violently punctured by sudden bursts of frantic, rhythmic swing.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre emerged in the damp, moss-covered basements of the Pacific Northwest, where the "Puddle-Jumpers" subculture thrives. These enthusiasts, known for wearing thrifted yellow rain slickers over vintage Victorian evening wear, host "Migration Rituals"—all-night listening parties where they attempt to synchronize their collective breathing to the erratic, quacking swing beats. They treat the corrupted cassette as a sacred text, believing the "Barking Duck" signifies a bridge between the frantic modern world and the restorative, deep-earth frequency of 432Hz.

Instruments: Detuned semi-hollow electric guitars, vintage brass synths (re-pitched to 432Hz), modular-processed duck-call samples, and brushed snare kits.

For Fans Of: The Go! Team, Stereolab, Penguin Cafe Orchestra, and Mouse on Mars.

